// Makes the sounds of the spells, the items and the heroes, the voices of the heroes and the music, with ElevenLabs: the
// sound effects API for the sounds, text to speech for the voices, and its music API for the soundtrack. Each is written
// as an .ogg to data/sounds/ (the music to data/music/), where the game plays it by its name (core/sound.sd), and the
// .wav of make_sounds.py that it replaces is removed.
//
//     make_audio                          what isn't there yet, or has changed
//     make_audio --force                  all of it again
//     make_audio sfx                      only the sounds (or: voices, music)
//     make_audio music.quiet              only the pieces of one soundtrack
//     make_audio ability.hex voice.hugo_cast_1        only these
//     make_audio --list                   what it would make, with the prompts and the lines
//     make_audio --adopt                  takes the files that are there as made from the prompts of now, without making
//                                         anything, e.g after the prompts were made by another version of this
//
// Built like the game: saad run tools/make_audio.sd on Windows; on Linux, saad build tools/make_audio.sd --elf and the
// link.sh of the Saad repository (it uses https, so OpenSSL). It needs an ElevenLabs API key in ELEVENLABS_API_KEY,
// unless a proxy adds it (as in a Claude Code cloud session; net.http goes through the proxy of https_proxy), and ffmpeg.
//
// How it sounds is all in this file: every spell belongs to a family, whose words come into each of its prompts, so
// spells of a kind sound like each other (smoke and shadows hiss and breathe, ice crackles, time ticks and runs
// backwards), and every hero has a voice, with its lines for when it comes onto the field, casts, gets across, is run
// over, gets an enemy run over, and is told to move. The sounds are trimmed and made as loud as each other, and the voices are pitched and
// put in a room for their hero, e.g a deeper ogre or a ghost in an echo. A hero that doesn't talk, Frosty the snow golem,
// makes noises instead, with the sound effects API: its lines say what each noise is. There are a few soundtracks to choose
// from, each a style of its own whose pieces share a little band, so they go together like the movements of one piece: the
// game plays the pieces of one at a time, with silence between. What all of them keep to is written down at MusicBrief.

import net.http;
import crypto;
import json;
import fmt;
import os;
import time;
import math;

Api       :: "https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1";
Model     :: "eleven_multilingual_v2";
Sounds    :: "data/sounds";                 // From the folder of the repository, where it is run
MusicFolder :: "data/music";
CacheFile :: "data/sounds/.elevenlabs.json"; // What each file was made from, so one whose prompt changed is made again
MusicModel :: "music_v1";

//
// Sound effects
//

// The feel of a kind of spell, which comes into every prompt of the kind
Family :: struct {
    name:  string;
    words: string;
}

Families :: [
    Family{"stealth", "soft breathy whoosh of dark smoke and shadow, hissing vapor, muffled and mysterious"},
    Family{"arcane", "witchy magic, sparkling arcane shimmer, fairy-tale fantasy"},
    Family{"mind", "dreamy hypnotic shimmer, warbling chimes, slow and eerie"},
    Family{"time", "clockwork ticking and a reversed swoosh, time bending, magical"},
    Family{"ice", "crisp crackling ice and frost, glassy crystal tinkles, cold wind"},
    Family{"storm", "electric crackle and rumbling thunder, wind"},
    Family{"traffic", "street sounds, car horns, tyres and brakes"},
    Family{"motion", "fast athletic movement, whoosh of air"},
    Family{"force", "heavy physical impact, punchy and weighty"},
    Family{"magnet", "deep magnetic hum and metallic resonance"},
    Family{"craft", "workshop and tools, wood and metal"},
    Family{"care", "gentle and soothing"},
    Family{"tourist", "a cheerful tourist with a camera and a phone"},
    Family{"item", "a cartoony game item, short and clear"},
    Family{"game", "a fantasy game interface sound, short and polished"},
];

// A sound: its name in the game, its family, what it is, and how long
Effect :: struct {
    name:    string;
    family:  string;
    what:    string;
    seconds: f32;
}

